BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1350546@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T111000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T111000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T124000
SUMMARY:Build your own software factory with context
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Joe Holdcroft\, Hamza Oza\, James Moss\n\nWorkshop ·
  Context & Skills Engineering\n\nMost teams using coding agents hit the sa
 me wall. The agent writes code quickly\, but someone still has to review i
 t\, check it for security problems and keep an eye on what it costs. A sof
 tware factory hands those jobs to agents too\, and what makes it work is c
 ontext: the instructions\, rules and skills that tell each agent how your 
 team does things.\n\nIn this hands-on workshop you'll build one. You'll us
 e the Tessl agent to work through it step by step. Along the way you'll se
 t up automated code review\, add security checks\, plug in reusable skills
  and get a handle on token spend. Where a piece isn't built in yet\, we'll
  show you how to approximate it with a simple agent loop.\n\nYou'll leave 
 with a working repo you can keep building on\, and an approach that carrie
 s over to whichever agent your team uses.\n\nWho it's for\n- Engineers and
  tech leads already using a coding agent such as Claude Code\, Cursor\, Co
 dex or Gemini. You should be comfortable with git and the terminal. No Tes
 sl experience needed.\n\nWhat to bring\n- A laptop with git\, Node.js and 
 your coding agent installed\n- A GitHub account\n- A repo you'd like to tr
 y this on (optional\; we provide a starter)\n\nWe'll give every attendee e
 xtra credits on the day\, so nobody runs out mid-session.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16f268be-4f18-4a8d-8727-a211e780687b@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T124000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T124000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T134000
SUMMARY:Lunch Break
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1350828@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T134000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T134000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T151000
SUMMARY:AI in Legacy Code: Small Steps\, Deterministic Tools\, Reusable Con
 text
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Llewellyn Falco\n\nWorkshop · Real-World Playbooks\n
 \nLevel: Beginner\n\nAI works well on greenfield code and falls apart on t
 he code most of us actually have. In this hands-on mob session\, we take a
  real legacy codebase and make it safe for AI\, live\, in small steps and 
 fast feedback loops.\n\nTogether we write the handful of CLIs every projec
 t needs: a fast test script\, a coverage script\, and format and lint tool
 s. We wire in Habit Hooks\, which turns linter findings into coaching an a
 gent can act on\, and use it to take on smells like duplication and long m
 ethods. We use deliberately fast\, low-thinking models\, because tiny step
 s and quick verification beat clever prompts.\n\nThen we extract knowledge
  into documents: product docs\, process docs\, and agent rules. We clear c
 ontext repeatedly and reload only what the agent needs from those docs\, s
 o the context is something we build and keep rather than something we re-e
 xplain every session.\n\n**You will leave able to:**\n- Build the basic CL
 Is a legacy project needs before an agent touches it\n- Wire in hooks that
  turn deterministic checks into guidance for the agent\n- Extract product 
 and process knowledge into docs and rules the agent can reload after every
  context clear\n
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8ddbdf27-9fe3-42b0-95c5-c64c56552304@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T151000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T154000
SUMMARY:Coffee break
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1323029@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T154000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T154000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T171000
SUMMARY:Building Custom Agents with the GitHub Copilot SDK
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Bruno Borges\, Jay Gordon\n\nWorkshop · Real-World P
 laybooks\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nIn this slide-free\, code and agent hea
 vy walkthrough\, we will build an agentic app with the GitHub Copilot SDK.
 \n\nWe will begin by understanding with the Copilot Agent Runtime harness 
 and then progressively build a museum exhibit curator agent. Attendees wil
 l create and manage sessions\, stream events\, expose application-owned da
 ta through a custom tool\, connect an MCP server\, and produce a structure
 d\, evidence-based report.\n\nGitHub Copilot SDKs include Java\, Node.js\,
  Python\, .NET\, Rust\, and Go support. Although each uses its language co
 nventions and type systems\, they all implement the same concepts.\n\nAtte
 ndees will see with a working application\, a practical understanding of t
 he agent and tool-execution loop\, and reusable patterns for building secu
 re\, testable Copilot-powered apps.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1364865@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T111000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T111000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T124000
SUMMARY:Managing AI Agents in Production
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Alexis Roberson\, Diane Phan\, Drew Gorton\n\nWorksho
 p · Software Factories & Dark Factories\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nA hands
 -on workshop for teams building software with AI agents. Attendees create 
 and deploy an agent\, then use LaunchDarkly AgentControl to manage prompts
 \, models\, and tools in production without redeploying. Along the way\, t
 hey’ll explore how to shape and evaluate agent behavior\, including in c
 oding-agent workflows\, and safely roll out changes as the agent evolves q
 uickly and with full assurance.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1335649@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T134000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T134000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T151000
SUMMARY:The Only Variable: Change Nothing but the Charter\, and Measure Wha
 t Moved
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Ian Johnson\n\nWorkshop · Context & Skills Engineeri
 ng\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nEveryone tells you to write a CLAUDE.md. Nobo
 dy shows you what it buys\, because nobody measures it. Here you measure i
 t\, on your laptop\, three times.\n\nYou get a repository you did not writ
 e\, three eras deep\, where the correct code is outnumbered ten to one. Yo
 u give your agent one ticket with no charter and watch it copy the worst t
 hing in the codebase. Then you add four guardrails one at a time\, probe e
 ach on its own\, and re-run the same ticket after each.\n\nThe deliverable
  is a matrix: eight checks a machine can settle\, and one it cannot\, whic
 h stays a question mark until a reviewer reads the diff. The hole in the s
 corecard is the whole point.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1363200@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T154000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T154000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T171000
SUMMARY:Context\, subagents\, MCP and memory: building agents that last
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Nicolas Carmont Zaragoza\n\nWorkshop\n\nA hands-on se
 ssion on building agents that hold up over long runs. We'll cover how to m
 anage context\, when to split work into subagents\, how to connect tools w
 ith MCP and how to give agents memory. You'll build one small agent step b
 y step with the Claude Agent SDK\, and leave with patterns you can use on 
 your own agents straight away.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1374981@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T111000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T111000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T124000
SUMMARY:Optimizing Agentic Workspaces: Skills\, MCP\, Custom Agents\, and S
 hared Context
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Brian Turcotte\n\nWorkshop · The Agent Enablement Pl
 atform\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nA productive coding agent needs more than
  a good prompt. It needs the right context\, tools\, instructions\, and bo
 undaries. This workshop explores how to set up your IDE or coding agent be
 fore you start writing code\, so it can work more consistently across task
 s\, projects\, and teams.\n\nLearn how to organize reusable skills\, conne
 ct tools and information through Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers\, de
 fine custom agents for specific responsibilities\, and establish configura
 tions that keep everyone working from the same expectations. We’ll also 
 cover practical guardrails: what agents can change\, when they should ask 
 for approval\, and how to check their work.\n\nThe focus is building a wor
 kspace you can maintain and expand without accumulating conflicting instru
 ctions\, unnecessary tools\, or agents with unclear roles.\n\nWhat you’l
 l come away with\n\n- A repeatable setup checklist for preparing an agenti
 c workspace before development begins.\n\n- A framework for choosing the r
 ight approach: when to use a skill\, an MCP server\, a custom agent\, or a
  simple instruction.\n\n- A structure for reusable skills and configuratio
 ns that can grow across projects and teams.\n\n- Clear agent responsibilit
 ies that make delegation and handoffs easier to manage.\n\n- Practical gua
 rdrails for tool access\, permissions\, code changes\, testing\, and human
  review.\n\n- A way to evaluate your setup using representative tasks\, id
 entify friction\, and improve it over time.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1334993@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T134000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T134000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T151000
SUMMARY:Stop Context Drift: Building Software with Deterministic Agents
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Tsvetan Tsvetanov\n\nWorkshop · Context & Skills Eng
 ineering\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAI coding agents are surprisingly good 
 at writing code. The problem is keeping them aligned with what you actuall
 y asked for as a project grows.\n\nIn this 90-minute hands-on workshop\, w
 e'll build an application with nWave and follow an idea all the way to wor
 king code. We'll see how nWave turns expectations into executable checks\,
  uses Agda and TLA+ to verify design decisions\, and runs implementation t
 hrough a deterministic execution system designed to prevent context drift 
 between intent\, design\, code\, and validation.\n\nRather than prompting 
 an agent and reviewing what comes back\, we'll work through an expectation
 -driven development loop where each step produces constraints for the next
 . Along the way\, we'll examine the generated code\, tests\, and verificat
 ion artifacts to understand what the system can guarantee — and where hu
 man judgment still belongs.\n\nYou'll leave with a working application and
  a concrete model for building agentic development workflows where correct
 ness doesn't depend on an agent remembering the right context.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1341997@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261102T154000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T154000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T171000
SUMMARY:The Developer's Guide to an Agentic Git Forge
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Joseph Katsioloudes\, Peyton Montei\n\nWorkshop\n\nCo
 ding agents help us ship fast\, but they’ve introduced a new challenge: 
 ensuring developers can understand\, trust\, and maintain agent-generated 
 code.\n\nThat challenge now shows up across the software development lifec
 ycle:\n\n1. Context is scattered across multiple sessions. 2. Developers h
 ave more code to review and less context to review it with.\n3. Code hosts
  face more automated activity than they were designed for.\n4. Agents burn
  tokens just trying to understand the codebase.\n\nSolving these problems 
 requires a new workflow and mindset for working with agents. In this hands
 -on workshop\, you’ll learn how to preserve context across sessions\, re
 duce wasted tokens\, and review agent-generated changes with the context y
 ou need to merge confidently.\n\nThis workshop is for engineers\, tech lea
 ds\, and reviewers of all experience levels who want to work more efficien
 tly with coding agents.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
