BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1369552@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T090000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T092000
SUMMARY:Welcome to AI DevCon
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Simon Maple\n\nConference Session\n\nLivestreamed for
  virtual attendees
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1324623@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T092000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T092000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T095000
SUMMARY:Building a software factory you can trust.
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Guy Podjarny\n\nKeynote · Agent Governance\, Securit
 y & Trust\n\nLivestreamed for virtual attendees\n\nBuilding a software fac
 tory isn’t the hard part — trusting it is. As AI systems take on more 
 of the development lifecycle\, the real bottleneck shifts from capability 
 to confidence and control. \n\nIn this talk\, we’ll explore a practical 
 path from skills\, to loops\, to a software factory: building up autonomy 
 in stages\, with the structure\, feedback\, and guardrails needed to trust
  each step. We’ll look at what makes that factory trustworthy\, and how 
 our role shifts from writing every line to steering a system we can genuin
 ely rely on.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1341006@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T100500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T100500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T103500
SUMMARY:Context != Context: One Window\, Different Lifecycles
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Birgitta Boeckeler\n\nConference Session · Context &
  Skills Engineering\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nWorkflow instructions\, coding c
 onventions\, tickets\, wiki pages\, and observability data all end up in t
 he same context window\, and we call all of it "context." To the model\, i
 t's all tokens shaping the next prediction. For the people who write\, mai
 ntain\, and rely on context\, these tokens differ a lot in where they come
  from\, how much authority they carry\, and how they can go wrong. Some ar
 e deliberately authored and quality assured. Others emerge as a byproduct 
 of work and are dynamically retrieved and reconciled from organizational s
 ystems.\n\nThis talk discusses how these differences change the ways that 
 each kind is created\, checked\, and delivered to the agent\, and that tre
 ating them alike can lead to using unsuitable tools and processes. You wil
 l leave with a clearer idea of the types of context you have available for
  your coding agents\, and how to best maintain them.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c614a14e-6120-4962-90d3-40eb81b2e790@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T103500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T103500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T110500
SUMMARY:Coffee break
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1365736@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T110500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T113500
SUMMARY:Models Alone Aren't Enough: Engineering Context as a Graph for Huma
 n + Agent Teams
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Alan Braun\n\nConference Session · Context & Skills 
 Engineering\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nLivestreamed for virtual attendees\n
 \nAI capability is advancing faster than organizational productivity. Fron
 tier models have moved from handling tasks that take humans minutes to tas
 ks that take hours. But making individuals more productive does not automa
 tically make teams and companies more productive\, especially when work sp
 ans functions\, systems\, and dependencies.\n\nCollaboration has always re
 quired transferring context. An engineer and a product manager need to sha
 re requirements\, technical constraints\, and past decisions. Human-to-age
 nt and agent-to-agent collaboration extend that challenge: a coding agent 
 assigned a Jira issue needs the relevant requirements\, related bugs\, dep
 endencies\, and rationale. That context lives across Slack and Teams actio
 n items\, Google Docs requirements\, Jira epics and workflows\, meeting no
 tes\, and bespoke systems.\n\nConnected context can improve both agent per
 formance and efficiency. In this talk\, we share how Atlassian approaches 
 this with a graph that connects context sources and the relationships amon
 g people\, work\, and decisions\, so agents get relevant context without b
 rute-force searching every tool. We walk through a recorded benchmark of t
 he same agent answering the same question with siloed tool access versus g
 raph-based context\, and show how we score answers with a blind LLM judge 
 on groundedness\, accuracy\, comprehensiveness\, and usefulness. Our inter
 nal benchmarks suggest graph-based context improves response quality while
  reducing token costs.\n
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1356104@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T114500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T114500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T121500
SUMMARY:Building When Code is Cheap and Your Time is Not
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Aidan Cunniffe\n\nConference Session · Standardizati
 on & Reuse at Scale\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nLivestreamed for virtual att
 endees\n\nHot take: many of us are trying to build software factories by o
 ptimizing the outer loop. What if the most important work is actually insi
 de the four walls of our applications?\n\nWe can now build software faster
  than we can understand it. This session explores how purpose-built framew
 orks\, DSLs\, and other abstractions once too expensive to justify can hel
 p us optimize for review and maintainability—compressing implementation 
 into clear intent and eliminating repeated judgment\, not just repeated co
 de.\n\nWhen writing code was expensive\, we optimized for reuse. When unde
 rstanding code is expensive\, we should optimize for comprehension.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1349722@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T122500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T122500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T125500
SUMMARY:Building a harness that keeps up with the latest models
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Nicolas Carmont Zaragoza\n\nConference Session · Con
 tinuous Evaluation & Optimization\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nLivestreamed f
 or virtual attendees\n\nEvery new model changes what a good harness looks 
 like. The prompts\, guardrails and workarounds that made the last one work
  can hold the next one back.\n\nI work with AI-native startups at Anthropi
 c to keep their harnesses in step with each new frontier model\, so they g
 et the best out of it on their use cases from day one.\n\nThis talk is abo
 ut building a harness that keeps up: how to spot what a new model no longe
 r needs\, test what changed fast\, and ship the upgrade in days\, not mont
 hs. Practical tips and tricks\, straight from the field.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f97f7243-20e3-4e00-a92f-5656f38c70b6@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T125500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T125500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T135500
SUMMARY:Lunch Break
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1342462@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T135500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T135500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T142500
SUMMARY:Security in GitHub Agentic Workflows
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Landon Cox\n\nConference Session · Agent Governance\
 , Security & Trust\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nLivestreamed for virtual atte
 ndees\n\nGitHub Agentic Workflows is like a team of street sweepers that c
 lean up messes in your repo. These teams significantly improve repo hygien
 e and quality\, but CI jobs like Agentic Workflows largely run out of sigh
 t and out of mind. How do you put guardrails on agents that have access to
  your repository and the internet? Will you be wondering if your agents re
 ad documentation from a sketchy website or pushed a commit containing an A
 PI token? What if they decide to add spammy comments to every open issue?\
 n\nOur approach to security in Agentic Workflows is guided by three design
  principles: (1) isolate agents from secrets\, (2) control agent inputs ba
 sed on repo-maintainer endorsements\, and (3) stage and vet all repo updat
 es. This talk will discuss how we apply those principles and the biggest c
 hallenges we see on the horizon.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1324898@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T143500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T150500
SUMMARY:An Architectural Approach to Regenerative Software
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Chad Fowler\n\nConference Session · Context & Skills
  Engineering\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nLivestreamed for virtual attendees\
 n\nPhoenix Architecture is a way of thinking about software in a world whe
 re implementations can be rewritten faster than they can be understood. As
  AI shifts the economics from maintenance to regeneration\, the real archi
 tectural questions move upstream: what must remain stable\, what can safel
 y change\, and what breaks humans when it breaks at all. This talk offers 
 a set of lenses (not answers) for reasoning about durability\, trust\, and
  coherence in long-lived systems. Attendees should leave with new instinct
 s\, sharper questions\, and a sense that some of our deepest assumptions a
 bout software may need to be revisited.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1323421@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T151500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T151500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T154500
SUMMARY:Your Agent Just Wrote a Tool—and Ran It. You Need More than a San
 dbox.
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Cornelia Davis\n\nConference Session · Agent Governa
 nce\, Security & Trust\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nLivestreamed for virtual 
 attendees\n\nAI agents are already well entrenched in the traditional soft
 ware delivery lifecycle: they build\, review\, test\, and deploy code\, ye
 t even as we automate more and more\, we are still talking about a control
 led build-test-deploy cycle.\n\nBut what happens when an agent writes and 
 executes new code not as a deliverable\, but as a tool for accomplishing i
 ts current goal? There is no pull request\, deployment pipeline\, or human
  checkpoint. This is testing in production in its most literal form.\n\nTh
 e DevOps movement made testing in production viable by creating safety net
 s: containers\, progressive delivery\, observability\, and rapid rollback.
  Runtime code generation by autonomous agents needs its own safety net\, a
 nd a sandbox is only the beginning. A sandbox controls where code runs\; i
 t does not fully control what that code can do.\n\nIn this demo-heavy sess
 ion\, we’ll examine the execution model required for agents to safely cr
 eate and run code without human intervention. Generated code must remain i
 nside the host agent’s governed context: inheriting its tool permissions
 \, policy constraints\, and relevant event history\, without acquiring cap
 abilities the agent itself does not possess.\n\nWe’ll introduce an execu
 tion environment built around these principles\, explore its architecture\
 , and share practical patterns for making runtime code generation observab
 le\, auditable\, and bounded.\n
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
