BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1340105@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T161500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T161500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T164500
SUMMARY:Scaling Agentic Engineering at Zalando
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Bartosz Ocytko\n\nConference Session · Standardizati
 on & Reuse at Scale\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nLivestreamed for virtual att
 endees\n\nIn the session\, I will be presenting Zalando's journey to enabl
 e agentic engineering for >2000 engineers. The session will open with the 
 observed effects of AI in coding on: engineers\, codebase quality\, and pr
 ocess bottlenecks. Key wins and enablers will be shown\, such as: customer
 -facing chat bots\, LLM-assisted (mass) migrations\, risk-based PR assessm
 ents. Further\, foundational pieces for enterprise enablement will be shar
 ed: platform investments (proxy\, agent platform\, skills\, readiness scan
 ning)\, tool integration & governance\, and enterprise guardrails that mak
 e adoption safe. The session will end by promoting mechanisms for bottom-u
 p knowledge sharing & upskilling that have proven their value at scale.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1332817@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T173000
SUMMARY:The Future of AI-Powered Factories
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Steve Yegge\n\nKeynote · Standardization & Reuse at 
 Scale\n\nLivestreamed for virtual attendees\n\nGet ready for a provocative
  closing keynote packed with AI-fuelled ideas from Steve Yegge\, the creat
 or of Gas Town and Beads.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1369553@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T180000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T210000
SUMMARY:🎉 Conference Party
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Social\n\n📍 Carreau Club\, Brooklyn\, New York\n\nThe talks 
 might be over\, but the fun is just getting started!\n\nJoin us at Carreau
  Club for an evening of drinks\, great conversations\, and a chance to unw
 ind with the AI developer community. Catch up with familiar faces\, make n
 ew connections\, and keep the conversations going beyond the conference fl
 oor.\n\nGood vibes\, great people\, and plenty of AI talk. See you there! 
 🍻
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1348430@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T100500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T100500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T103500
SUMMARY:Beyond the Pilot: Enabling AI Coding in Regulated Enterprises
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Mashrur Hossain\n\nLeadership Track · Standardizatio
 n & Reuse at Scale\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nGetting an AI coding tool int
 o developers’ hands is only the beginning. In large\, regulated enterpri
 ses\, sustained adoption depends on how well it fits the organization’s 
 security controls\, developer environments\, engineering practices\, and e
 xpectations for measurable value.\nDrawing on my experience helping large 
 financial-services organizations adopt AI-assisted development\, I’ll sh
 are practical lessons about turning initial interest into everyday enginee
 ring use. We’ll explore how to identify focused\, valuable use cases\; a
 lign security\, platform\, and engineering teams\; and build hands-on enab
 lement around the work developers actually need to do.\nWe’ll also exami
 ne where adoption stalls: workshops without a clear follow-through plan\, 
 environment and access issues that interrupt momentum\, and metrics that t
 rack activity without demonstrating outcomes. These experiences show why e
 nterprise enablement is an ongoing organizational capability\, not a one-t
 ime training event.\nAttendees will leave with a practical approach to sca
 ling AI coding adoption: establish technical readiness\, define ownership\
 , develop internal champions\, connect learning to real workflows\, and cr
 eate feedback loops that improve both the developer experience and the rol
 lout itself.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1336211@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T110500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T113500
SUMMARY:From individual coding tools to development coding platforms
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Massimo Re Ferre'\n\nLeadership Track · Real-World P
 laybooks\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nThis session explores the opportunities and
  challenges observed in large organizations as they navigate their ongoing
  journeys. These journeys span a broad spectrum from individual\, in-line 
 coding assistance to ambitious attempts at building team-level software fa
 ctories. I'll focus on a few key dimensions that shape these opportunities
  and challenges\, beginning with the concept of trust\, along with the eco
 nomics I've observed while working with AWS customers and engineering orga
 nizations at Amazon. I'll also share what has worked well as a solution or
  mitigation to specific challenges\, and what hasn't. Expect more anecdote
 s than data.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1349384@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T114500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T114500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T121500
SUMMARY:From AI Coding to the Agentic SDLC: Booking.com’s Journey Toward 
 Autonomous Software Delivery
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Valerio Panzica\n\nLeadership Track · Leadership\n\n
 Level: Intermediate\n\nOver the last few years\, AI has rapidly changed ho
 w we think about software engineering at Booking.com.\n\nOur journey start
 ed with experimentation: giving engineers access to emerging AI coding too
 ls\, learning where they created real value\, and understanding how develo
 pers actually changed the way they worked.\n\nAs the technology matured\, 
 so did our approach. We moved from experimenting with individual tools tow
 ard building shared capabilities\, scaling adoption across the engineering
  organization\, and embedding AI more deeply into the software development
  lifecycle.\n\nIn this talk\, I’ll share the evolution of our GenAI for 
 Engineering journey at Booking.com: what we tried\, what we learned\, how 
 our strategy changed\, and why we are now moving beyond coding assistants 
 toward an Agentic SDLC.\n\nI’ll also share our vision for the next phase
 : an increasingly autonomous software delivery lifecycle where specialized
  agents participate across planning\, design\, implementation\, validation
 \, operations\, and observation\; reusable golden paths connect those capa
 bilities into end-to-end workflows\; and engineers progressively move from
  executing every step themselves to supervising and orchestrating AI-power
 ed delivery systems.\n\nThe destination is not software development withou
 t engineers. It is a fundamentally different engineering system\, where hu
 mans increasingly define intent\, constraints\, and outcomes while agents 
 take on more of the execution.\n
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1340875@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T122500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T122500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T125500
SUMMARY:Who Gets the Keys? Trust and Authority in Software Factories
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Paul Duvall\n\nLeadership Track · Agent Governance\,
  Security & Trust\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAs software factories become m
 ore autonomous\, the hard problem shifts from generating code to deciding 
 who holds authority over what ships. Human review of a diff was never that
  control\, and at agent speed it can't keep up.\n\nThis talk follows a cha
 nge from customer intent to a running system across three feedback latenci
 es: seconds in the agent loop\, minutes in CI\, and hours in production. A
 long the way\, untrusted input tries to steer the agent\, a pattern alread
 y behind real-world incidents.\n\nYou'll learn which pipeline controls sti
 ll hold\, which ones change\, and which agent-specific checks to add. We'l
 l separate authorship from authority. Agents can write code\, tests\, and 
 fixes. The open question is who decides whether any of it ships.\n\nYou'll
  leave knowing how to verify intent\, not just behavior\, where human judg
 ment still belongs\, and who should hold the keys to production.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1318692@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T135500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T135500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T142500
SUMMARY:Botsitting Doesn't Scale: How to Navigate the Autonomy-Trust Gridlo
 ck in Real Engineering Orgs
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Nicholas Arcolano\n\nLeadership Track · Agent Govern
 ance\, Security & Trust\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nEvery engineering organi
 zation is trying to maximize two things for their agents right now: autono
 my and trust. The problem is that the standard way to build each one under
 mines the other.\n\nTrust gets built through supervision\, by watching the
  agent work\, reviewing everything\, and always keeping a human in the loo
 p. That's how most teams operate today\, and it sets a hard cap on scaling
 \, as humans struggle to supervise more than a handful of agents directly.
  Autonomy is what actually scales\, but it means trusting work you didn't 
 watch happen. A micromanaged factory isn't efficient\, and an untrusted fa
 ctory can't be pointed at anything that matters.\n\nI've been studying thi
 s gridlock using data from the Jellyfish platform\, which combines AI agen
 t telemetry with software engineering workflow data across 1\,000+ compani
 es and over a million developer-weeks of AI tool usage.\n\nThe trust probl
 em is real: currently about 40% of the pull requests written by autonomous
  agents don’t get shipped. The teams closing that gap aren't botsitting 
 more closely. They're replacing supervision with verification\, using revi
 ew discipline and better scaffolding to build trust on artifacts instead o
 f attention. The loudest workflow debates of 2026 – from killing the pul
 l request to mandatory human review to real-time steering – are all comp
 eting answers to the same problem.\n\nThis session will share lessons on h
 ow to navigate the autonomy-trust gridlock drawn from real production data
 \, and a way to tell which trust-building strategies scale and which ones 
 will become the next major bottleneck. Supervision is how you build trust 
 in an agent. Verification is how you build trust in a factory.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1373427@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T143500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T150500
SUMMARY:1\,000 Coding Agents\, One Main: Leading the Shift to Concurrent De
 livery
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Patrick Debois\n\nLeadership Track · Software Factor
 ies & Dark Factories\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nOrganizations are building 
 software factories: fleets of coding agents\, automated validation\, chang
 es that merge themselves. But what happens when you actually turn the fact
 ory on? Hundreds of agents start working at once\, and their work collides
  as it comes back together into one codebase\, one release\, and one perso
 n’s understanding of the system. Code gets written faster\, but delivery
  doesn’t speed up with it.\n\nTwo coordination problems sit underneath: 
 \n- Agents must work alongside each other without undoing each other’s w
 ork. Or having turf wars on the code base.\n- And people\, whose attention
  is now the scarce resource\, must still direct the work and decide what s
 hips\, even when no person wrote the change.\n\nThe next advantage isn’t
  faster agents. It’s delivering when they all work at once.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1346589@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T151500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T151500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T154500
SUMMARY:What Breaks First On The Way To Machine Speed: A Macro and Micro Vi
 ew
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Dan Lorenc\n\nLeadership Track\n\nSoftware has three 
 speeds: how fast problems get found\, how fast they get fixed\, and how of
 ten you can ship. Nobody owns all three. Finders\, maintainers\, vendors\,
  and operators are each sewn into the same chain\, upstream of some and do
 wnstream of others\, and they’re reaching machine speed at different tim
 es. Finding got there first. Fixing is close behind. Shipping is set by yo
 ur tests and your change control\, and it isn’t moving. Whoever falls be
 hind the stage in front of them becomes the bottleneck\, and everything pi
 les up on them.\n\nThis talk looks at where that pile lands\, at two scale
 s\, from both the development and security sides. At the macro scale\, sec
 urity breaks in the slow middle: upstream patching at a crawl has quietly 
 protected everyone downstream\, and that dam is going. Development breaks 
 the other way: migration costs have collapsed\, and the teams that can’t
  absorb constant small breaking changes stall while their neighbors sprint
 . At the micro scale\, security breaks at the design of a single component
 \, and development breaks at a single migration\, where compatibility now 
 means whether an agent can make the change cheaply.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1349717@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T161500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T161500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T164500
SUMMARY:Why Your CTO Is Suddenly Writing Code
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Rob Zuber\n\nLeadership Track · Leadership\n\nLevel:
  Intermediate\n\nRob has spent 25 years building a mental model of how sof
 tware gets made\, and AI broke it. Suddenly he couldn't tell where his eng
 ineers were stuck\, and he wasn't sure his instincts still counted for any
 thing. So he went back to the code\, and not as a side quest\, because the
 re's no substitute for a gameday and no way to understand how your enginee
 rs live without living it. What does it look like when a leader gets that 
 close to the work and still has to make the calls in a market that moves t
 his fast? \n\nYou'll hear an honest account of what he got wrong and what 
 was humbling. If you lead\, you'll know when to dig in\, when to shortcut 
 the hierarchy and decide\, and when to get out of the way. If your leaders
  have started asking odd questions or poking at your tools\, you'll know w
 hat to expect from them right now and how to help them catch up.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1324093@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T100500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T100500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T103500
SUMMARY:Dealing with Agentic Coding Risk: What Breaks Going From 1 Agent to
  10
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: James Moss\n\nTools-in-Action · Real-World Playbooks
 \n\nAdding more coding agents does not multiply output cleanly. It creates
  a coordination system with new failure modes: agents editing the same cod
 e\, maintenance work triggering more maintenance work\, tasks competing fo
 r shared state\, and failures that no single agent appears to own.\n\nThis
  session is a field report from scaling an internal software factory from 
 individual agent runs to concurrent\, connected workflows. We will examine
  what breaks when agents stop operating in isolation\, including conflicti
 ng changes\, recursive triggers\, non-deterministic execution\, and recove
 ry paths that create more work than the original failure. The focus is not
  generic multi-agent theory. It is the practical risk of letting multiple 
 coding agents act on the same repositories\, queues\, and delivery process
 .\n\nAttendees will learn where orchestration needs explicit ownership\, i
 solation\, idempotency\, and stop conditions\, plus how to design systems 
 that detect collisions and recover without silently compounding the proble
 m.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1369990@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T110500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T113500
SUMMARY:Hack the planet? Patch the planet.
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Geoffrey Huntley\n\nTools-in-Action · Agent Governan
 ce\, Security & Trust\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nWhen a certificate authority i
 s compromised\, a compression library is vulnerable\, or a package is pois
 oned\, patching the one copy you own is easy. Patching every copy buried i
 n transitive dependencies is not. Each ecosystem\, base image\, and runtim
 e carries its own\, and each needs its own patch\, release\, and redeploy.
 \n\nMost teams open a pull request upstream and wait. The maintainer never
  signed up to be their vendor\, is already drowning in AI-generated PRs\, 
 and owes them nothing under an “as is” license. Or they fork it\, then
  own the build pipeline\, the artifact registry\, and a divergence that st
 arts rotting the moment they stop looking.\n\nYour supply chain moves at t
 he speed of the slowest maintainer you depend on. That’s a hostage situa
 tion.\n\nThis talk shows another way. Define a single source of truth for 
 every dependency across the stack\, from the developer laptop to CI\, cont
 ainer images\, agent sandboxes\, and the operating system that enables an 
 agent to patch the world. \n\nOne prompt to an AI agent answers “are we 
 affected?” across the fleet. One patch\, written once by an agent\, fixe
 s everything that depends on the broken component. No fork. No waiting. No
  PR queue.\n\n
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1335505@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T114500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T114500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T121500
SUMMARY:Did It Actually Improve the Software Factory?
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Chris Poulter\n\nTools-in-Action · Continuous Evalua
 tion & Optimization\n\nLevel: Expert\n\nA software factory can produce mor
 e code\, more retrospectives and more improvement issues without getting a
 ny better. How do we turn experience into a justified change\, and establi
 sh whether it helped?\n\nDrawing on practical failures and design decision
 s from building Nucleus\, this talk explores the improvement lifecycle: di
 scovering opportunities\, testing proposed changes and deciding which to p
 ut into practice. A lesson might lead to better instructions\, a determini
 stic check or an earlier human decision. Improvement can mean simplifying 
 the process as readily as adding automation.\n\nThe critical part comes af
 ter adoption: did the change actually improve the software factory? We’l
 l examine how to judge its effect on software quality\, delivery time\, co
 st and human effort\, and whether the evidence justifies keeping it.\n\nTh
 is is not a software factory overview or a product demonstration. It is ab
 out engineering the process by which a factory discovers\, tests and retai
 ns improvements\, without assuming it automatically gets better after ever
 y run. Attendees will leave with an improvement lifecycle they can apply t
 o their own development workflows.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1341862@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T122500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T122500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T125500
SUMMARY:The Tool Got Smarter. Did You?
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Ahmed E. Hassan\n\nTools-in-Action · Real-World Play
 books\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nAI coding is often presented as a technology s
 hift: better models\, longer context windows\, and more capable agents. Bu
 t the real change is much deeper. This is a new way of thinking about how 
 we understand problems\, discover solutions\, build software\, evaluate ev
 idence\, and take responsibility for what we ship.\n\nThe tool changed. Th
 e question is: did we?\n\nWhen AI can explore a codebase\, propose a desig
 n\, implement a feature\, write tests\, and review its own work\, being a 
 good engineer can no longer mean simply producing good code. We must becom
 e better at framing problems\, expressing intent\, directing work\, questi
 oning convincing answers\, learning through rapid experimentation\, and ex
 ercising judgment when no test can tell us what is right. Leaders must als
 o rethink what they reward\, how people learn\, and what meaningful AI ado
 ption actually looks like.\n\nThis is not a talk about which coding agent 
 to buy or which prompt to use. It is about the human and organizational tr
 ansformation required when the tool becomes dramatically more capable. Thr
 ough relatable stories\, uncomfortable failures\, and seven practical prin
 ciples plus one essential constraint\, we will explore how to benefit from
  AI without becoming passive\, superficial\, or dependent on it.\n\nThe ce
 ntral question is no longer simply\, “How do I use this new tool?” It 
 is\, “Who do I need to become now that the tool can do so much of what u
 sed to define my work?”
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1340999@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T135500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T135500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T142500
SUMMARY:How SpaceXAI Uses Cursor
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Shivam Jindal\n\nTools-in-Action · Real-World Playbo
 oks\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nSoftware delivery at SpaceXAI no longer look
 s like a person writing a PR and waiting on a queue. A majority of our mer
 ged PRs are fully done by cloud agents\, end to end. In nine months\, PR v
 olume grew 5x. That is how a small team ships Origin\, a new take on GitHu
 b\, and Grok Bot\, a general-purpose worker\, without slowing down the cor
 e product.\n\nFaster coding just moved the bottleneck. Review\, CI\, secur
 ity\, and ownership still had to happen\, and they could not scale as a hu
 man queue. So we automated those loops\, and left people on the work that 
 actually needs judgment.\n\nMost of it runs in the background. Long-runnin
 g cloud agents sit on their own machines for hours: a Slack thread\, incid
 ent\, a ticket\, or a red build starts one\, it investigates and reproduce
 s\, then it opens a PR. Other agents review for bugs and security and size
  the risk. If CI fails\, another agent reads the logs and failed tests and
  tries a fix. Low-risk PRs can self-drive through merge. \n\nThis is the f
 actory we run internally.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1340065@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T143500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T150500
SUMMARY:Minimize Review and Increase Trust: A Verification-Aware Process fo
 r Agentic Programming
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Fernanda Graciolli\, Nada Amin\n\nTools-in-Action · 
 Agent Governance\, Security & Trust\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nOne of the m
 ost powerful uses of AI in software engineering is automation. But true au
 tomation with minimal human oversight requires a high degree of trust in t
 he system’s ability to generate correct output. Given that long-running 
 processes anchor the next output to its own prior outputs\, an error along
  the way compounds quickly and may render the entire session or project un
 usable. We present a “verification-aware” process of programming with 
 AI that embeds various verification methods — with formal verification a
 s the grounding method. This process is flexible enough to accommodate too
 ling preferences\, organization-specific policies\, and cost/risk toleranc
 e.\n\nWe’ll focus on practical patterns for verification-aware programmi
 ng inspired by functional programming\, how verifier feedback can guide ag
 ents to reason about correctness\, and how to ensure informal and formal s
 pecifications don’t drift. The process and methods result in a considera
 ble decrease in review burden while increasing confidence in output correc
 tness.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1293077@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T151500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T151500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T154500
SUMMARY:Rebuilding the Software Lifecycle for Coding Agents and Humans
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Joseph Katsioloudes\, Peyton Montei\n\nTools-in-Actio
 n · Context & Skills Engineering\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nCoding agents 
 are writing code faster than ever\, picking up issues and stories with gro
 wing autonomy. Yet context is fragmenting across chats\, prompts\, session
 s\, and branches\, leaving agents stateless and developers without a share
 d understanding of intent\, decisions\, and learning. And the human develo
 per still sits at the center of the pull request\, reviewing code and sugg
 estions.\n\nIt's time for a new software development lifecycle for a world
  of agent-to-human and agent-to-agent collaboration\, one that collapses p
 lanning\, coordination\, design\, implementation\, review\, and deployment
  into a continuous loop. And one that removes bottlenecks while preserving
  quality\, trust\, and developer happiness. Join Joseph Katsioloudes for a
  demo-filled talk on what this loop looks like in practice today\, where i
 t breaks\, and what you can start changing in your own workflow.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1317015@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261103T161500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T161500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T164500
SUMMARY:In the loop\, not in the dark: staying in control in the era of dar
 k software factories
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Alex Gavrilescu\n\nTools-in-Action · Context & Skill
 s Engineering\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nLast year I built Backlog.md\, an open
  source tool that captures your intent\, splits it into small markdown tas
 ks\, and lets you follow your agents' progress on a TUI Kanban board. A ye
 ar and 300\,000 agent-written lines later\, my projects had quietly turned
  into a small dark factory: software getting built with nobody really revi
 ewing it. Task tracking told me what the agents did\, but not what my syst
 em had become. I am no longer the owner of my code.\n\nToday I am building
  Groma.md to fix this. Groma keeps a real-time architectural blueprint of 
 your project's domain as the source of truth\, and tasks become the means 
 to reach the blueprint's next iteration. Imagine UML without the fear that
  it goes stale\, where you can watch exactly which parts of the system you
 r agents are modifying.\n\nWhether you are adding an order management syst
 em to a brownfield codebase or starting a new project from scratch\, you d
 esign it in Groma and let the agents write the code\, while you keep an ey
 e on the overall architecture and stay in control of the meaningful decisi
 ons.\n\nIn this talk I will take a large open source repository and map it
  into a Groma blueprint live on stage\, to show you how to become the owne
 r of your code again.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:21d25df0-512e-431b-8a88-31c698dd97c5@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T080000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T090000
SUMMARY:Registration and Breakfast
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1371030@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T090000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T092000
SUMMARY:Welcome to AI DevCon
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Simon Maple\n\nConference Session
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1346550@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T092000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T092000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T095000
SUMMARY:The AI Chop Shop: Everything You Ship Will Be Stolen
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Erik Meijer\n\nKeynote · Agent Governance\, Security
  & Trust\n\nFor fifty years\, the software industry has thrived on the fac
 t that code is scarce. Proprietary vendors protect it by keeping the sourc
 e secret. Open source protects it with licenses that attach obligations to
  copying.\n\nAI destroys the assumptions underneath both.\n\nA contemporar
 y coding model needs very little to reverse engineer years of human engine
 ering effort. Hand it a binary\, an API\, an obfuscated bundle\, or just e
 nough observed behavior\, and it can infer what the system does\, recover 
 its structure\, and emit a fresh implementation. The result is clean\, doc
 umented\, idiomatic\, and has no traceable connection to your "IP." The fu
 nctionality survives. The provenance doesn't. Trade secrets stop being sec
 ret the moment you ship. Copyleft has nothing left to grab onto.\n\nWelcom
 e to the software chop shop. Your software comes in through the back\, get
 s stripped for parts\, and leaves with new paint and the VIN filed off.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1322896@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T100500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T100500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T103500
SUMMARY:Version Your Factory\, Not Just Your Code
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Daniel Meppiel\n\nConference Session · Standardizati
 on & Reuse at Scale\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nMost agentic software factor
 ies begin as a collection of prompts\, scripts\, and agent skills. That wo
 rks until a model changes\, the team adopts another harness\, or a securit
 y rule requires changes across the entire factory.\n\nWhat if we treated t
 he factory like the software it produces?\n\nIts capabilities would become
  composable modules. They would have versions\, dependencies\, tests\, own
 ers\, and release notes. Once factory capabilities become versioned compon
 ents\, they can be tested\, reviewed\, shared\, replaced\, and composed. T
 eams could upgrade one part without rewriting the rest. Factories could be
  improved through the same open-source practices that built the rest of ou
 r software ecosystem.\n\nAgent Plugins 1.0 gives us a starting point by ma
 king Skills and MCP servers easier to move between harnesses. The factory 
 sits above them: the process remains stable while Copilot\, Claude Code\, 
 Codex\, and other harnesses can be swapped underneath it.\n\nThe same prin
 ciple applies to governance. A factory can accept reusable and swappable r
 ules about tools\, risk\, and required checks. Governance determines which
  versions and capabilities may run.\n\nI’ll demonstrate a prototype fact
 ory built from versioned modules\, run it through two harnesses\, replace 
 one capability without changing the workflow\, and show how guardrails as 
 code allow agents to ship autonomously without crossing their limits.\n\nA
 gent Plugins 1.0 is not a complete factory specification. That is exactly 
 why this conversation and more open standards are needed.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c1f115a8-46da-499d-8156-3f9ae4fdeec5@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T103500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T103500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T110500
SUMMARY:Coffee break
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1293900@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T110500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T113500
SUMMARY:From Page to Dossier — Using Agents to Your Advantage
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Paul Stack\n\nConference Session · Real-World Playbo
 oks\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nAn alert fires at 3am. Your on-call engineer ope
 ns their laptop\, checks Grafana\, tails the logs\, traces what deployed i
 n the last hour\, diffs the config changes\, checks whether an upstream de
 pendency is having its own incident\, searches Slack for the last time thi
 s happened\, and reads half a runbook before they even understand what's w
 rong. Forty minutes of context-gathering before a single useful decision g
 ets made.\n\nAn AI agent can do every one of those steps. Not better than 
 your engineer — but without losing focus at 3am\, or cutting corners onc
 e the investigation gets repetitive. Wire one into your alerting pipeline.
  When an alert fires\, it pulls metrics\, correlates logs\, traces deploym
 ents and config changes\, finds related past incidents\, reads the runbook
 \, and assembles a dossier that lands alongside the page. Instead of spend
 ing forty minutes gathering context\, the engineer starts with it.\n\nVend
 ors are starting to ship their own versions of this — Honeycomb Canvas\,
  Dash0\, and others. But a vendor's agent knows about the vendor's platfor
 m. It doesn't know your deployment pipeline\, your change management syste
 m\, or the five other tools your investigation actually touches. This talk
  shows how to build your own\, wired into your stack\, producing a dossier
  that's exactly what your organisation needs. Same structure every time\, 
 same sections\, same layout — because a sleepy engineer at 3am needs a r
 eport they can read on autopilot\, not a surprise.\n\nThe agent does not m
 ake the call. It gathers the evidence and presents it. Your on-call engine
 er decides what happens next — because they have taste\, judgement\, and
  the kind of contextual understanding you only get from knowing your syste
 ms\, your team\, and your business. The goal isn't to take the human out o
 f the loop. It's to stop wasting their judgement on context-gathering and 
 point it at the decision that actually matters.\n\nReal demos\, real alert
 s\, real monitoring data. You're not being replaced. You're arriving with 
 the first forty minutes already done.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1306269@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T114500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T114500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T121500
SUMMARY:Dark Factory Considered Harmful
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Ray Myers\n\nConference Session · Real-World Playboo
 ks\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nNow that the value of coding agents in practi
 cal software engineering has been established\, many people assume that go
 ing fully agentic is the logical conclusion. This culminates in a vision c
 alled the Dark Software Factory - we eventually turn the lights off and ne
 ver see code again.\n\nThis talk takes the opposite position: Dark Softwar
 e Factory is flawed as a general recommendation\, and being "more agentic"
  is not always better. The vision of virtual employees isn't engineering-d
 riven. It's fueled by fundraising narratives\, investor pressure\, and exe
 cutives justifying layoffs.\n\nThe proposed alternative is The Show-Me Met
 hod. Decide based on evidence which parts of your application are "code wo
 rth writing" and which should be left to agents. Evolve the boundaries ove
 r time in support of business needs. Aim for high leverage with minimal ri
 sk.  This involves awareness of your sources of risk and "defense in depth
 " practices that build on the principles of Continuous Delivery.\n\nAnti-P
 attern 1: Engineering malpractice. If the rush to signal AI adoption under
 mines our responsibilities to security\, privacy\, and reliability\, that 
 is harmful. We have a profession and are trusted to uphold a reasonable st
 andard of care\, such as the ACM Code of Ethics.\n\nAnti-Pattern 2: Unorga
 nized labor. Many software engineering teams fear mismanagement and layoff
 s but are not positioned to respond with collective action. The first step
  is learning how this works in other trades.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1324091@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T122500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T122500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T125500
SUMMARY:Harness Engineering in Practice: The Loops That Make Agents Trustwo
 rthy
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Dru Knox\n\nConference Session · Agent Governance\, 
 Security & Trust\n\nA coding agent is not just a model. It is the model pl
 us the system around it: context\, permissions\, checks\, reviews\, evalua
 tions\, and maintenance loops that determine whether its output can be tru
 sted.\n\nThis session moves beyond defining an agent harness and shows how
  one works in practice. We will follow a real reliability loop through its
  full lifecycle: the failure it was designed to catch\, how the team encod
 ed the right boundary\, what happened when the loop fired\, and how the re
 sult changed the harness itself. Along the way\, we will examine the trade
 -offs between strict controls and useful autonomy\, why one-off guardrails
  decay\, and how teams can turn production failures into reusable improvem
 ents.\n\nAttendees will leave with a practical model for designing harness
  loops that catch problems before merge\, improve with use\, and reduce th
 e amount of manual supervision needed as agent adoption grows.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fe186ebc-263f-4d5b-8160-90d50f85803e@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T125500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T125500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T135500
SUMMARY:Lunch Break
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1338349@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T135500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T135500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T142500
SUMMARY:Your Software Factory Already Has a Name: Continuous Delivery
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Bryan Finster\n\nConference Session · Leadership\n\n
 Level: Intermediate\n\nMore organizations are attempting to build a softwa
 re factory. Most are building the assembly line and ignoring what feeds it
 .\n\nIn 2014\, my team at Walmart went from quarterly releases to 5+ deliv
 eries a day by asking one question\, "Why can't we deliver today's work to
 day?" and fixing every answer. \n\nIn 2025\, I hit the same walls with cod
 ing agents: vague requirements\, review bottlenecks\, and no shared defini
 tion of "good" all resulted in rework and delayed delivery. I found that t
 he same principles that fixed it for people also worked for agents. \n\nTh
 is talk maps Continuous Delivery onto the agentic software factory and the
  principles and critical practices that drive good outcomes. We'll also co
 ver what I'm learning introducing this inside an enterprise and why softwa
 re delivery is still a people problem. \n\nIf your plan is "add agents\, g
 o faster\," come find out why that produces garbage faster\, and what to f
 ix first.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1333403@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T143500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T150500
SUMMARY:The "Evolution of the Intent"
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Akhil Sharma\, Sudipta Mohapatra\n\nConference Sessio
 n · Context Engineering\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nTraditional conversatio
 nal assistants relied on a strictly finite list of hardcoded "intents" lik
 e GetWeather or BookFlight. In 2026\, modern LLM-based assistants utilize 
 State-Space Planning to navigate ambiguous\, complex user objectives. This
  session explores how replacing rigid intent schemas with flexible "Goal D
 efinitions" empowers agents to dynamically pivot when a user says\, "Actua
 lly\, let's do that on Friday instead." Learn how to construct robust\, no
 n-linear conversational workflows that handle real-time context changes an
 d mid-task adjustments without breaking.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1323542@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T151500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T151500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T154500
SUMMARY:BYOB - build your own benchmark to evaluate and post-train coding a
 gents for your team
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Joe Tyler\n\nConference Session · Continuous Evaluat
 ion & Optimization\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nA new coding model tops SWE-B
 ench on Monday. Should you roll it out to your team on Tuesday? A new tool
  or MCP server is released. Should you use it straight away? Public benchm
 arks can’t answer these questions\, because none of them were built for 
 your code.\n\nWithout knowing how the models were trained\, it’s difficu
 lt to know whether labs have “benchmaxxed” on popular open benchmarks 
 like SWE-Bench or Terminal-bench. Even more recent benchmarks like DeepSWE
  which is written by hand to avoid contamination\, or FrontierCode which i
 ncludes a measure of production code quality\, are not built for the repos
 itories or workflows you’ll be using AI coding tools for in your day-to-
 day.\nIn short: these benchmarks are all relevant\, but none of them have 
 been tailored for your use cases\, and therefore none can be trusted to gi
 ve a realistic prediction of how a coding model or agent will perform in y
 our domain.\n\nAt Sonar\, as the leader in independent verification of AI-
 generated and developer-written code\, our research team has invested sign
 ificant time and resources into understanding the qualities and defects of
  code generated by AI coding tools and models. This is done to answer ques
 tions the community keeps asking: “Which coding model writes code that i
 s easiest to maintain?” or “Where should I focus my code review when u
 sing this model?”. We use these tools ourselves\, so we built our own cu
 stom benchmark\, which allows us to independently verify the performance o
 f each new model\, agent\, tool or configuration in workflows that reflect
  our developers’ AI coding tool usage. We do this to compare these optio
 ns and recommend tooling\, but also as an evaluation tool for our custom m
 odels to hill climb on through post-training.\n\nIn this talk\, Joe Tyler\
 , Lead AI Researcher at Sonar\, will look at the important aspects when de
 signing evaluations to be indicative of production quality code\, before w
 alking through how we built a custom benchmark\, and what we learnt when c
 omparing leading open and closed source coding agents.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5275a535-dfe1-493f-aeee-c1bee5fdc7c6@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T154500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T154500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T161500
SUMMARY:Coffee break
LOCATION:All rooms\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1347200@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T173000
SUMMARY:A Dream Factory for an Instrument Made of Light
LOCATION:52A\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Geert Bevin\n\nKeynote · Context & Skills Engineerin
 g\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nGlinster is a musical instrument for Apple Vis
 ion Pro\, iPhone and iPad that you play with your bare hands. It began as 
 a prototype I never had time to finish\, and it launches on November 3\, b
 uilt in two months by one person and Claude Code running in auto mode.\n \
 nI ran it as an experiment. I read very little of the code\, on purpose\, 
 to find out whether the processes and methods we built for programming wou
 ld hold up without it. An instrument is an unforgiving place to try that: 
 it has to look\, feel and sound right at the same moment\, and every mista
 ke shows up in front of you as a glitch\, a missed note or a gesture that 
 feels wrong\, whether or not the tests pass.\n \nThis is a look inside tha
 t factory. How do you talk to it when what you want is a feeling? What goe
 s wrong when nobody is reading the code? And what is left for you once the
  factory takes the treadmill? Expect real stories from the transcripts\, a
 n honest answer on whether the experiment held\, and a live performance to
  close.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1333143@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T100500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T100500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T103500
SUMMARY:We Stopped Reading Diffs and Started Verifying Intent
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Ankit Jain\n\nConference Session · Organizational en
 ablement\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nOur team merges 4x more pull requests t
 han we did a year ago. Almost all of that code is AI-generated\, human ass
 isted. We tried scaling code review to match — more reviewers\, AI revie
 w tools\, stricter checklists — and none of it worked. What worked was s
 topping the fight over diffs entirely and building a verification layer ar
 ound *intent*.\n\nThis is the story of how one engineering team replaced t
 he PR review bottleneck with an intent-driven verification workflow: captu
 ring what a change *should* do before a single line is generated\, encodin
 g team standards as reusable invariants\, and letting deterministic checks
  — not tired reviewers — produce the evidence that a change is safe to
  ship.\n\nI'll walk through the specific practices that made the differenc
 e — especially how we turned years of recurring review comments into mac
 hine-enforceable invariants — and share the results\, the failures\, and
  what we'd do differently.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1314200@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T110500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T113500
SUMMARY:Your agent isn't stupid\, it's blind.
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Ran Aroussi\n\nConference Session · Context & Skills
  Engineering\n\nLevel: Expert\n\nDistill\, don't store: what we learned bu
 ilding memory for company-scale agents\n\n\nMost agent memory is a pile of
  raw text with a similarity search on top. It works in a demo and falls ap
 art the moment two sources disagree\, a decision gets reversed\, or one pe
 rson isn't allowed to see what another person said.\n\nWe built the memory
  layer differently\, and I'll go straight past memory 101 into the design 
 decisions that actually moved the needle.\n\nNo raw storage. The retrieval
  unit is a distilled claim with provenance\, not a chunk. Humans do not re
 member every word of conversations\, but rather remember what established 
 – so we built the system that way\, and I'll show what that does to accu
 racy and to context budget.\n\nBelief has a timeline. What did the organiz
 ation believe last quarter\, what superseded it\, and when. Most systems j
 ust accumulate contradictions and let the retriever pick a winner at rando
 m. Detecting the contradiction is a feature\, not an error.\n\nPermission 
 survives distillation\, which is the hard part. Distillation destroys the 
 source boundary that access control lives on. A claim assembled from four 
 systems with four different ACLs has to answer differently for different p
 eople. This is the piece I've seen almost nobody address.\n\nAgents write 
 to it too. They're a producer\, not just a consumer – and letting an age
 nt write into shared knowledge poisons it fast unless the schema is human-
 authored and the instances are machine-populated.\n\nI'll show the archite
 cture\, the benchmark methodology\, our scores on LoCoMo\, LongMemEval\, a
 nd BEAM (1M/10M)\, and the design decisions I'd reverse. White paper publi
 shed alongside the talk.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1346202@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T114500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T114500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T121500
SUMMARY:We Gave AI Memory. What About Awareness?
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Pari Patel\n\nConference Session · Context Engineeri
 ng\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nA coding agent may remember the entire conversati
 on while still giving its user an explanation they cannot follow or reques
 ting a decision without enough context. Remembering what happened is only 
 part of supporting the person doing the work.\n\nPari Patel\, founder of b
 rain-sensing wearable company Zone\, explores how developer tools could ac
 count for the human receiving their output. Here\, awareness means sensiti
 vity to the user’s task and interaction needs. Through a proposed coding
 -assistant workflow\, she will examine explicit preferences\, task context
 \, and user feedback as inputs for adjusting explanation depth\, pacing\, 
 and handoffs.\n\nThe session also considers what neural sensing might even
 tually contribute\, with a clear distinction between current capabilities 
 and proposed applications. Attendees will leave with practical ways to des
 ign more responsive interactions while giving users control over adaptatio
 n.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1353543@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T122500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T122500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T125500
SUMMARY:One Harness API to Rule Them All
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: George Fahmy\n\nConference Session · Agent Orchestra
 tion\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nMost teams have stopped building agents fro
 m scratch. They build on Claude Code or Codex instead\, and the interestin
 g work has moved up one level: tools that run many agents\, review bots\, 
 long running background tasks\, factories that turn issues into PRs.\n\nTo
  build any of that\, you need to program the harness. Start it\, stream wh
 at it does\, steer it\, approve its tool calls\, resume or fork a session\
 , run it in a sandbox. Each lab ships an SDK for its own harness only. So 
 every tool either commits to one lab or writes its own glue for each harne
 ss.\n\nI think that's a missing layer\, and I've been trying to build it a
 s an open-source Harness SDK. In this talk I'll share where it stands\, th
 e earlier attempts that didn't work\, how harnesses differ in ways that ma
 ke them hard to abstract\, and the problems we're still working through.\n
 \nI'll end with a few demos of tools I built with Harness SDK.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1338686@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T135500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T135500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T142500
SUMMARY:Memory Without Deterministic Coordinates Is Just Hearsay
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Veljko Kovac\n\nConference Session · Context Enginee
 ring\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nCoding agents now work on the same reposito
 ries across engineers and across weeks\, and every session ends with a fin
 ding. Those findings are saved as sentences in private memory files\, with
  nothing that says which function they concern or which version the agent 
 was looking at. Two agents can investigate the same function a month apart
 \, and no tool can tell their notes are related. This talk shows why the o
 bvious fixes fail for the same reason\, and why the one that works needs a
  deterministic code graph: stamping every finding\, as it is written\, wit
 h the identity of the function and the version the agent was looking at\, 
 computed the same way on every machine. Using a deterministic code graph a
 s the coordinate system and a memory layer that stores findings against it
 \, findings become shareable and comparable across agents and repositories
 \, and summaries over them become counts rather than guesses. 
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1362487@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T143500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T150500
SUMMARY:Code Readiness for Agentic Development
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Tracy Lee\, Coston Perkins\n\nConference Session · S
 oftware Factories & Dark Factories\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nCoding agents
  succeed when the codebase supports autonomous work. This talk explains ho
 w reproducible environments\, enforceable standards\, deterministic checks
 \, evidence-based review\, protected merging\, and production monitoring p
 repare code for agents from issue to release. Each step leaves the enginee
 r’s hands only when a script or independent agent can validate it. In on
 e month\, this approach helped a team merge 334 of 373 agent pull requests
  on the first try\, with only four requiring human-written code.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1346089@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T151500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T151500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T154500
SUMMARY:Stop Writing Skills. Start Training Them: Self-Improving Skills for
  Coding Agents
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Shrey Shah\n\nConference Session · Context Engineeri
 ng\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nWe write skills for our coding agents: how to
  migrate code\, how to write tests\, how to review PRs in our team's style
 . Then we forget about them. Six months later the skill is stale and the a
 gent keeps making the same mistakes. The usual fix is to add a line by han
 d and hope\, with no check that it helped\, or that it didn't quietly brea
 k another task.\n\nThis talk treats a skill like a model checkpoint and tr
 ains it. The agent runs a batch of tasks. A second model reads the failure
 s and proposes a small\, bounded edit. The edit is scored on held-out task
 s the editor never saw\, and git decides: better\, keep the commit\; not b
 etter\, git reset. Rejected edits are logged and fed back\, so the editor 
 stops repeating them. The loop runs overnight\, survives crashes\, and use
 s the agent CLI you already have.\n\nI'll walk through two real runs from 
 my open-source trainer. One scores SQL with a deterministic script. The ot
 her uses a pinned\, structured LLM judge for a skill with no single right 
 answer: a skill that reviews other skills\, which went from 0.39 to 0.61 o
 n a test set nobody looked at. I'll show what failed along the way: broad 
 rules that fixed one task and regressed others\, and an average-based gate
  that threw away real gains until I switched to a paired\, same-seed gate.
  And the two tests every trainer should pass: recovering a deleted rule\, 
 and accepting nothing on pure noise.\n\nWhat you ship is still one SKILL.m
 d file. No runtime cost. The agent just follows better instructions.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1325473@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T161500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T161500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T164500
SUMMARY:Automating Design Quality in the Dark Factory
LOCATION:The Landing - Ground Floor\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Hamza Oza\n\nConference Session · Context & Skills E
 ngineering\n\nAs coding agents produce more implementation work\, design q
 uality can become a late-stage review problem. A system may generate funct
 ional software quickly while still drifting from interaction patterns\, ac
 cessibility standards\, content guidance\, or the product’s visual langu
 age.\n\nThis session explores how design teams can move quality checks ear
 lier by encoding parts of their practice into the software factory itself.
  We will examine which design decisions can become reusable context\, rule
 s\, examples\, and automated checks\, and which still require human judgme
 nt. The talk will also cover the organizational shift for designers: from 
 reviewing every generated screen to shaping the systems that influence man
 y agent decisions before code is written.\n\nAttendees will leave with a p
 ractical framework for deciding what to automate\, how to keep encoded gui
 dance current\, and how to use review findings to improve the factory rath
 er than repeatedly fixing the same class of issue.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1319045@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T100500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T100500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T103500
SUMMARY:Graph Engineering in Reverse
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Benjamin Gruenbaum\n\nTools-in-Action · Continuous E
 valuation & Optimization\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nGraph Engineering invol
 ves creating knowledge and task graphs. Creating a good knowledge graph in
 volves multiple interesting steps like entity extraction\, data parsing\, 
 noise elimination and graph algorithms. It turns out that if we start with
  a really good knowledge graph and do everything a company brain does in r
 everse we get a really good synthetic company with clones (twins) of all t
 he common connectors we need. The missing piece was being able to tap into
  Eon's exabyte scale backup for metadata.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1312658@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T110500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T113500
SUMMARY:Stop Babysitting Your Agents
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Jack Wotherspoon\n\nTools-in-Action · Real-World Pla
 ybooks\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nFor a while\, using agents meant managing
  them. You stood them up\, watched them closely\, and kept them going. You
 r role slowly became managing agents and watching a screen.\n\nThis talk i
 s about the switch: stop managing agents yourself\, and start using Manage
 d Agents to get the job done.\n\nI’ll show how you can spin up Gemini Ma
 naged Agents to automate daily tasks\, but also how to build them into you
 r product and applications. \n\nWhat changes when the agent is a feature o
 f the product instead of something you have to run on the side? You no lon
 ger are a babysitter and can get back to being in control.\n\nYou’ll lea
 ve with a simpler picture of that move\, from managing agents to using the
 m within your product.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1325124@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T114500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T114500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T121500
SUMMARY:AI Agents Are Your New Users
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Patrick Chanezon\, Jay Gordon\n\nTools-in-Action · C
 ontinuous Evaluation & Optimization\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAI coding ag
 ents are becoming users of your APIs\, documentation\, tools\, and service
 s\, and they don’t behave like human developers. Experiences that seem i
 ntuitive to people can send an agent down the wrong path\, but traditional
  developer-experience testing won’t reveal why.\nAgent behavior can be e
 valuated at scale: you can run the same developer scenario in a sandbox an
 d inspect every action\, tool call\, error\, and outcome. This gives teams
  a way to test how changes to their guidance affect an agent’s ability t
 o complete real tasks.\nIn this talk\, we’ll introduce Project SCOPE\, a
 n open-source system for evaluating developer scenarios across combination
 s of models\, coding-agent harnesses\, and extensions such as skills\, MCP
  servers\, and instruction files. Drawing on twelve months of experiments\
 , we’ll share how SCOPE helped the Azure Cosmos DB team improve develope
 r experience by identifying gaps in agent skills and documentation\, refin
 ing that guidance\, and evaluating the results.\nYou’ll leave with a pra
 ctical approach for measuring agent experience\, diagnosing failures\, and
  improving the skills and documentation that developers and their coding a
 gents depend on.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1333986@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T122500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T122500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T125500
SUMMARY:Operating Coding Agents at Scale
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Nandini Singhal\n\nTools-in-Action · Continuous Eval
 uation & Optimization\n\nLevel: Beginner\n\nRolling out coding agents acro
 ss an engineering organization requires evidence about how they are used\,
  whether they improve engineering work\, and how each change affects quali
 ty\, latency\, and cost.\n\nAt Datadog\, we combine gateway telemetry\, no
 rmalized agent-session traces\, task evaluations\, and software-delivery o
 utcomes. Gateway data captures model usage\, errors\, latency\, and cost. 
 Session traces show context and tool use\, file changes\, tests\, and retr
 ies. Controlled evaluations compare models\, harnesses\, and configuration
 s on the same tasks. Staged rollouts test those changes with engineers and
  monitor for regressions before broader availability.\n\nTogether\, these 
 signals guide rollout decisions\, platform defaults\, and cost controls wi
 thout treating token volume\, session count\, or tool calls as measures of
  productivity.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1337975@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T135500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T135500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T142500
SUMMARY:More Team Automation\, Less Agent Autonomy
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Katharina Dzialets\n\nTools-in-Action · Organization
 al enablement\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nAt JetBrains\, we used preconfigur
 ed environments to delegate feature-sized work on the IntelliJ monorepo. W
 hat began as a setup for a few AI champions was soon adopted by entire tea
 ms\, exposing how difficult it was to share and scale.\n\nEach task starte
 d from a hand-written prompt that rarely carried all the necessary context
 . A generated change could pass every formal check and still be conceptual
 ly different from what the team needed\, while increasingly detailed promp
 ts only made the agent’s behavior harder to predict. In practice\, teams
  got more value from handing off smaller\, repetitive steps. Instead of ma
 king each agent run larger and more autonomous\, we needed to make the wor
 k smaller\, more predictable\, and reusable.\n\nThat led us to build a pla
 tform where teams compose larger workflows from shared automations with pr
 edefined context\, permissions\, tools\, and network access. We reused the
  preconfigured monorepo environments and secure proxy layer from the earli
 er setup. Today\, more than 100 teams use the platform\, with 935 automati
 ons across the SDLC.\n\nIn this talk\, I’ll explain why delegating entir
 e features did not work well for teams\, how smaller shared automations ma
 de delegation more predictable\, and what 935 automations reveal about the
  work JetBrains teams choose to delegate to coding agents.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1335485@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T143500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T150500
SUMMARY:The Software Factory of Factories
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Baruch Sadogursky\n\nTools-in-Action · Standardizati
 on & Reuse at Scale\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nTessl built its own software
  factory. For a company working on how AI builds software\, that's a reaso
 nable investment. You're probably not Tessl\, though. How much of the mach
 inery around your agents should you be building and maintaining yourself?\
 n\nIn this session\, Baruch will argue that platform engineering should gi
 ve teams a way to assemble factories for their own work\, drawing on organ
 izational knowledge they don't have to collect again for every agent. We'l
 l look at how a shared context lake feeds governed workflows: a deployment
  workflow\, for example\, can use service ownership to route approval to t
 he responsible team.\n\nBut teams will also want agents to write and chang
 e those workflows. That puts factory construction itself under the platfor
 m's governance\, including permission to change the steps where humans int
 ervene. We'll examine where teams need that freedom and which security and
  permission decisions belong outside the factory they're building.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1319847@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T151500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T151500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T154500
SUMMARY:Software Factories for the Software of One
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Matthias Luebken\n\nTools-in-Action · Agent Governan
 ce\, Security & Trust\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nToday’s software factori
 es are designed for distribution: a team of humans and agents builds an ap
 plication\, then many people use it. AI changes that economic model. When 
 an employee can create a useful application for themselves\, we can no lon
 ger afford to treat every application as a bespoke artifact requiring cent
 ralized design\, approval\, and long-term maintenance. The next software f
 actory should be able to produce and operate a long tail of small\, task-s
 pecific applications: software for one person\, one workflow\, or one deci
 sion. This does not mean abandoning engineering standards or enterprise go
 vernance. It means building them into the factory itself: identity\, share
 d data\, guardrails\, secure capabilities\, and a sandbox.\n\nIn this talk
 \, we explore the architecture behind software of one: lightweight isolate
 d compute\, durable state\, capability-based access to shared systems\, an
 d clear paths for sharing\, reuse\, promotion\, and retirement. Including 
 throwing code away and rebuilding from scratch. The goal is not to replace
  shared applications. It is to evolve how we build software so that everyo
 ne can safely become a builder.
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1323260@sessionize-dcbso6w5
DTSTAMP:20261104T161500Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T161500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T164500
SUMMARY:If AI can build anything why does every app have a f*&*ing sidebar?
LOCATION:The Landing - Lower Level\, Industry City Building 5\, Brooklyn
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Sawyer Hood\n\nTools-in-Action · Real-World Playbook
 s\n\nLevel: Intermediate\n\nIf Claude Code and Codex can build an interfac
 e in seconds\, why do their own interfaces look like Microsoft Outlook 200
 9?\n\nIn this talk\, I’ll cover the brief history of coding agent interf
 aces\, why they look the way they do\, and what’s stopping us from build
 ing truly malleable software: applications whose interfaces can adapt\, re
 configure\, and even build themselves around what the user is trying to do
 .
URL:https://tessl.io/devcon#agenda
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
